# Cloudflare Pages response headers.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ models
# 16.1 MB across two files, previously served as `max-age=0, must-revalidate`.
# The bytes were spared on a 304, but only while the browser still held them —
# and unhashed multi-megabyte files are the first thing a phone evicts, so
# repeat visitors were re-downloading them. Every visit also paid two
# revalidation round-trips before inference could start.
#
# These filenames are content-stable: the vector contract pins the exact model
# revision, so det_500m.onnx and w600k_mbf.onnx cannot change meaning without
# the whole index being rebuilt. That makes them safe to freeze.
#
# IF A MODEL IS EVER SWAPPED, RENAME THE FILE. Editing one in place would leave
# clients on a year-old copy, producing embeddings that silently disagree with
# the index — the exact failure tools/golden exists to catch.
/models/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# Vite fingerprints everything in /assets, so these are safe to freeze too.
# Includes the 12.9 MB onnxruntime wasm binary.
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin

# Cloudflare Access authenticates the REQUEST; it does nothing about the
# authenticated page being embedded in someone else's. /admin can create events,
# rewrite bib data and fire CI jobs, so a clickjacked frame is worth closing.
# Scoped to /admin rather than /*: event pages are meant to be embeddable, and
# that is how a running club drops one into their own site.
/admin
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'none'

/admin/*
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'none'

# ----------------------------------------------- why there is no COOP/COEP
# onnxruntime uses wasm threads only when `self.crossOriginIsolated` is true,
# which needs `Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin` and
# `Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy: require-corp`. Both were implemented, and the
# hang they appeared to cause was traced and fixed — Vite never emitted
# `ort-wasm-simd-threaded.mjs`, so Emscripten's pthread pool waited forever on
# workers that could not boot. Pointing `ort.env.wasm.wasmPaths` at a directory
# holding that script makes threads work in ~65 ms.
#
# They are still not enabled, because with the bug fixed threads made no
# difference. Measured warm, 10-core, Angkor album: 992/993 ms on one thread vs
# 989/994 ms on four, of which ~280 ms is the API round-trip. The client does
# one detection and one embedding on a single image — there is nothing to
# parallelise. Cold start was much worse with threads (11,990 ms vs 1,902 ms).
#
# So this buys no speed and costs a constraint: under COEP every cross-origin
# subresource must send CORP or it silently fails to load. Not worth it.
#
# One piece of groundwork is kept: the Worker sends
# `Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: cross-origin` on /r2/*, which costs nothing
# today and is required the day COEP is ever switched on.
#
# BEFORE ENABLING COEP, note that photos are cached in browsers for a year as
# `immutable`. Anyone holding a copy fetched before that CORP header shipped
# would see broken photos until it expired. Those copies do now carry CORP, but
# verify that before flipping the switch — if any predate it, the asset URLs
# need a one-off cache-buster, which is expensive: it forces every thumbnail
# back through the Worker's R2-fetch-and-cache path, and that is what the
# CPU bill is made of.
#
# lib/face.ts pins numThreads to 1 unconditionally, so turning these headers on
# for some other reason cannot resurrect the hang.
